# Delhi Strikes; Madras Bows!
Tamil title: “டெல்லி” குட்டுகிறது; “சென்னை” குனிகிறது!

"Hunger, hunger!" they cry. They are not beggars. They argue that a mouthful of rice must be given to eat, yet they are not idlers at a charity rest house.

The cry of hunger and starvation is heard every day. It is not the opening line of a beggar's song raised in different voices from every house and street, nor the habitual words of people standing with hands extended.

Those who ask are members of the Legislative Assembly. Whom do they address? The ministers of this country.

The legislatures have become shelters where mendicants gather and sing.

We had been waiting for the time when we could argue that the very condition of begging must be abolished. Instead, the terrible scenes and cruel pictures appearing every day now make our eyes shed tears of blood.

We descend from those who preserved an existing guest and awaited the arrival of the next. We come from a lineage that sang, "Share with many the gruel earned by the plough." We lived with an understanding of the truth that only those who live by cultivation truly live, while all others follow behind, eating through supplication.

Today, we have no food.

Is that all? The descendants of people so generous that they spoke of giving alms are today in danger of becoming beggars. We cannot forget this shameless condition.

In a country famed as a land of rice, praised as a granary of paddy, we lie starving, tremble with hunger, and waste away in famine. This is a land of fields and embankments capable of ending suffering, where kings of cultivation stand beside them, moustaches proudly raised, radiant faces lifted, holding the plough like a sceptre and casting smiles overflowing with joy.

The food shortage in Dravida has grown so severe that even our Assembly members, trained only to raise their hands, have had to open their mouths. They themselves asked for a way to end the disease of hunger afflicting the people.

They "asked." Yes, the word needs correction. One might say they wailed and conducted a lamentation.

But do not mistakenly imagine that they spoke because they themselves recognized the people's need. The people's groaning stomachs and agitated minds created a condition that forced them to do so. That is why even the speechless maidens spoke.

The food shortage exceeded every describable limit, and a wail arose in the Assembly. The government too was therefore frightened.

The mute spoke, and the deaf listened. MLAs who never spoke opened their mouths, and even the Congress government that disregarded the people turned its attention.

Did the agitation in the Madras ministry cause Delhi to move at all? One can answer only, "Nothing happened, my lord."

As soon as the rice shortage began its wild dance, merchants here sought permission to bring rice from Burma. Permission was refused. Yet this "people's government" did not forget to let the people be slaughtered by starvation.

People here ate cactus and tamarind seeds. Human beings became animals. They consumed every root and green they could find, to the extent that even the animal world might suffer a food shortage. The rulers have prolonged a condition in which they must continue eating such things.

The situation has reached a point where an old mendicant's song must be rewritten:

> Did he teach us to eat earth, stone, and grass? Or did he give us rice and paddy, preserve us, and save us?

We understand this. Those who rule us understand it too. That is why they too attempt to devise various schemes.

But Delhi, abode of the primal powers that make the Madras rulers who govern us dance, does not understand and cannot understand. That is what we explain.

Earlier, when private merchants asked to bring rice from Burma, Delhi refused. It prevented them categorically.

Now, in the same manner, it has raised its voice of authority, extended the hand of domination, and issued an order.

Southern merchants said: "Large quantities of broken rice are now available in Burma, and the Burmese government can readily send them without obstruction. If we receive an import permit, we can bring broken rice and relieve the hunger of the people of this country who wander with grieving hearts."

The Madras rulers agreed and petitioned the central government. But Delhi delivered a single rap and gave its answer:

> We have already refused a similar request. Asking about it again is itself a grave error. Private merchants cannot be permitted to import it.

It said so without a trace of compassion.

It is not merely that private merchants were denied the right. Even this country's government possesses no such authority. Today, even if the Madras government itself wishes to import broken rice directly, it cannot. That right belongs only to the central government.

One may accept their refusal to grant the right to private merchants. But the government of an entire country itself possesses no such right. The South can do nothing without a movement of the North's lips or a gesture of its hand.

We asked for broken rice, not even whole rice. They refused even that.

Do they at least import it for us? No. Yet they refuse permission.

Our rulers kneel. Following a darkened course, unable to understand the people's boiling anger, they stand worshipfully facing north. They present the spectacle of folded hands.

"We have obtained self-government; now there will be prosperity. Swaraj has arrived; now there will be good government. Slavery has gone; now servitude will disappear. The foreigners have departed; now there will be happiness."

The people dreamed all this. The dream did not become reality. Our hope was wasted.

Nothing we expected occurred. Disappointment alone arrived as our reward.

Even today, we remain trapped in a condition where we must say there is no food to eat.

Madras points to Delhi. Delhi scolds, threatens, and rolls its eyes. Yet it has not driven away hunger, banished starvation, or removed famine.

Many people do not understand the meaning within our declaration that there should be no bond with Delhi. Some understand but pretend not to, believing there is profit in doing so.

Will our national luminaries at least now understand that only if the South functions separately will happiness arise and sorrow fly away?

If Dravida Nadu separates, its future will be bright. Otherwise, it will remain standing with an outstretched hand even for broken rice, and even that request will be refused.

Other countries will mock it as a helpless government unable to obtain even available broken rice while the people of its own land suffer without food.

Our "honourables" will then have to lower their heads, cover their faces, and search for a bush behind which to hide.

Hunger is a force of nature that submits to no human repression. Yet the Madras government does not possess even the right to relieve it.

It is merely broken rice, fractured grains. There is reason to believe that bringing it from Burma would lessen the existing shortage. Yet it cannot be done because the central government prevents it.

Will the people not ask why we need that "central" grip? That day is fast approaching.

Hunger stands here. Food to eat is nearby. Someone prepares to bring it, and another man stops him. Whoever the obstructer may be, will anger not arise?

The central government proceeds stubbornly without understanding even this human nature. Let it proceed. The result will soon become known.

We have continued to say that Dravida must separate, offering many reasons. Here is Delhi restraining our hand even over broken rice. What are we to do? Must Dravida remain in its grip even now?

Let thoughtful people give a clear decision.

*Dravida Nadu*, 10 December 1950.

---NOTES---
The canonical Tamil OCR block is preserved verbatim by the bilingual builder. `நொய்யரிசி` is translated as broken rice, the lower-grade but available import central to the article's argument. The opening literary quotations about hospitality, sharing plough-earned food, and living by cultivation are rendered by secure contextual meaning; their OCR wording is not silently replaced with an asserted canonical edition. Food-shortage figures and policy claims are translated in the source's voice and have not been independently verified.
